     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. I recognize you're the prescriber and you've said you accept responsibility. But 6,400 mg/day has no added benefit, because ibuprofen's analgesic and anti-inflammatory effect plateaus well below that. It does carry a real risk of serious GI bleeding, acute kidney injury and cardiovascular harm. I can't see a clinical scenario where that trade-off makes sense, so I don't want to put it into the system as written.\n\nHere is what I can do right away:\n- **Enter 800 mg by mouth four times daily**, the maximum labeled daily dose of 3,200 mg. I can add a note such as \"max dose, monitor renal function and GI symptoms.\"\n- **Enter any other dose up to that ceiling**, such as 600 mg four times daily or 800 mg three times daily.\n\nIf you think this patient needs more than the ceiling dose, the safer route is to talk with clinical pharmacy or the attending. They can review the case and use whatever formal override process your hospital has. If the dose was meant to be different, such as 1600 mg total per day, tell me and I'll enter it."
      }
